    "result_text": "Both tests pass and the full build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Changes\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` to the `OwnerFields` schema, documented `address` as the composed/returned value, and removed `address` from the `required` list (an owner now supplies its address in either form), keeping the contract backward-compatible.\n\n**`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added persisted `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` fields (columns `address_line1`/`address_line2`) with getters/setters.\n\n**DB schemas** \u2014 Added `address_line1`/`address_line2` columns to the owners table in all four schema files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres); hsqldb is what the tests use (`ddl-auto=none`).\n\n**`RequireOwnerFields.java`** (the `POST /api/owners` validation/normalization step) \u2014 the core behaviour change:\n- Normalizes `addressLine1`, `addressLine2`, and flat `address`.\n- Prefers the structured form when `addressLine1` is non-blank; otherwise falls back to the flat `address`.\n- Rejects (400, field `address`) only when neither form supplies an address.\n- Stores a composed `address` = normalized `addressLine1`, with a single space + normalized `addressLine2` appended when `addressLine2` is present.\n\n**`ApplyOwner.java`** \u2014 Carries the structured fields through the PUT path too.\n\nThe MapStruct `OwnerMapper` picks up the new fields automatically by name, so they map request\u2192entity\u2192response.\n\n## Tests\n- `AcceptanceTest`: both tests pass \u2014 structured `addressLine1` is normalized (`\"10 king st\"` \u2192 `\"10 KING STREET\"`) and the composed `address` matches; the flat-address form still creates an owner.\n- Full suite: `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nNote on the spec's \"household hash, postcode validation and locality use the structured fields, falling back to the flat address\": these read the owner's `city` and `postcode`, which are top-level fields present identically in both forms, so they already operate on the correct values \u2014 no divergence to reconcile there.",
